                <div class="col flex-basis-60">
                    <p>
                        To this day, Texas still has 3 dry counties in the state. Hays County, which borders the
                        Austin, Texas, area was also a dry county until fairly recently in the grand scheme of time.
                        During the early years of being a wet county, Dripping Springs was considered a small town,
                        with not much in the way of entertainment. By 2018 the landscape had completed shifted. Huge
                        destination breweries and distilleries could now be found through out the Texas Hill
                        Country.
                        <br><br>
                        Before Acopon Brewing Co. was born, John McIntosh and Dave Niemeyer had opened the first
                        craft beer bar in Dripping Springs, right on historic Mercer St. When they approached me to
                        help them develop an identity for their new brewery, we knew we needed something to stand
                        out. The common cliche for breweries is to name it after their local area, landmark, or history.
                        This makes sense from a
                        community perspective, you want to push your locality, but it is also fairly boring.
                    </p>
                    <p class="callout">
                        Acopon(ak’-o-pon); an obsolete English word that originated in the 16th century, primarily
                        referring to a health tonic, cordial, or restorative medicine.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        I recommended, instead, focusing on what actually makes them unique; traditional English Ales
                        served from a hand pump. This traditional style of beer led us down the path of searching for a
                        unique
                        Old English word, that resonated in some way with consuming a beverage. Thus, Acopon was born.
                    </p>
                    <p>With the name chosen, I explored a variety of ideas primarily dealing with health or healing
                        including alchemy, potions, and tonics; in the end however, we decided on a Plague Doctor. In
                        the times of the bubonic plague, most plague doctors were simply public servants and not doctors
                        at all. In that sense, a pint beer may restore your spirit, though it's not advocated to
                        actually be healthy for you, especially in excess.</p>
                    <p class="callout">
                        The theming has continued throughout their individual beers, from historical English callbacks,
                        to German folklore and Slavic mythology.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Beers such as “Gas Pipes”, named named after the street lamps of London before they were
                        electric powered, “Hans Trapp”, the Bavarian version of Krampus, and “Polenice”, a noon witch in
                        Slavic mythology. These themes not only help make the brewery stand out, but create a ton of
                        merchandising opportunities, which not only boosts the breweries brand awareness in the public,
                        but is a major revenue driver for small brewpubs.
                    </p>
